User: How do bank capital ratios affect credit supply elasticity?
Assistant: Bank capital ratios fundamentally reshape credit supply elasticity by addressing the inherent agency problems and externalities in financial intermediation. In the absence of binding capital requirements, banks exhibit excessively elastic credit supply during booms due to moral hazard from deposit insurance and too-big-to-fail expectations, followed by sharp contractions during downturns as information asymmetries intensify. Higher capital ratios reduce this procyclical elasticity by forcing banks to internalize more downside risk, leading to more stable credit provision across the cycle. Empirical evidence from the post-2008 regulatory reforms demonstrates that better-capitalized banks maintained lending relationships during stress periods while undercapitalized institutions exhibited severe credit rationing. The modest reduction in steady-state credit elasticity represents an efficient correction of market failures rather than a deadweight loss, as it eliminates socially excessive risk-taking and negative spillovers from bank failures.

